Good catch — one point: you're right to question it. If your API relies entirely on cookies for auth (req.cookies.access), you technically don't need to allow the Authorization header at all, since your server never reads that header anywhere in your code.

Here's the actual rule for allowedHeaders: it should only list headers your client actually sends and your server actually needs to receive. Since cookies are sent automatically by the browser (not manually attached as a header by your JavaScript code), Authorization serves no purpose in your current setup.

Your accurate config, matching what your app really does:

javascript
app.use(cors({
  origin: true,
  credentials: true,
  methods: ["GET", "PUT", "PATCH", "POST", "DELETE"],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type']
}))
Content-Type stays, since your fetch() calls do send that header (e.g., 'Content-Type': 'application/json' when registering/logging in).

Why this matters for an interview specifically — this is actually a sharp thing to bring up unprompted if asked about your CORS setup: "I only allow the headers my API actually uses. I removed Authorization since my auth relies on httpOnly cookies sent automatically by the browser, not a manually-attached bearer token header — including unused headers in allowedHeaders doesn't break anything, but it's imprecise and can misrepresent how your API actually authenticates."

One caveat worth knowing, though: if you ever build a mobile app or a non-browser client (like a React Native app, or another server calling your API) to use this same backend later, those clients typically can't rely on cookies the same way browsers do — they'd need to send the token via an Authorization header instead. So keeping Authorization in allowedHeaders now, even if unused today, is also a defensible choice if you're planning to support other client types eventually. It's a genuine tradeoff, not a clear-cut "right answer" either way — but for a pure cookie-only web API, removing it is the more precise, accurate choice.

Which framing do you want to go with — strictly accurate to current usage (remove it), or future-proofed for other client types (keep it)? Either is defensible in an interview if you can explain your reasoning either way.







