Yes — exactly that, one point: Content-Type: application/json is the label telling your server "read the body as JSON," and JSON.stringify(...) is what actually turns your JavaScript object into that JSON text in the first place.

They work as a pair — one prepares the data, the other labels it:

js
body: JSON.stringify({ Username, email, password })
// ↑ turns your object into JSON TEXT, like: '{"Username":"sarah","email":"sarah@test.com"}'

headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
// ↑ the STICKER saying "what I just sent you is JSON text — read it that way"

Without JSON.stringify(): your object would be sent in a broken/wrong format the server can't properly parse.

Without the Content-Type sticker: even if you did send proper JSON text, your server might not know to treat it as JSON — and express.json() middleware specifically looks for that sticker before it agrees to parse the body.

So both are needed together, doing two different jobs:

JSON.stringify() → converts the object into JSON text (the actual "package")
Content-Type: application/json → labels that package correctly (the "sticker")

