Good question, but there's a mix-up worth clearing up first: it's not the browser that reads/parses the JSON — it's your server (Express/Node). Let's separate the two roles clearly.

The browser's job: just send the data, correctly labeled. It doesn't "read" or interpret anything at this stage — it just packages up your object into JSON text (via JSON.stringify()) and slaps the Content-Type: application/json sticker on it before sending.

Your server's job: receive that data and actually parse/read it — this is where express.json() comes in:

js
app.use(express.json());

This middleware looks at the incoming Content-Type header. If it sees application/json, it knows to take the raw text body and convert it back into a real JavaScript object (req.body) that your controller code can use, like req.body.email.

So to directly answer "why should the browser read it like that" — the browser isn't reading it at all in this direction; it's just labeling what it's sending, so that whoever receives it (your server) knows how to correctly interpret it.

Now — "if the browser is reading it like that, definitely the frontend won't read it like that" — I think what you might be getting at is: does the frontend ever need to do the reverse (turn JSON text back into an object)? Yes, actually — and you're already doing this too, just going the other direction, when your frontend receives a response back from the server:

js
const res = await fetch(...);
const result = await res.json();  // ← THIS is the frontend "reading"/parsing JSON

res.json() is the frontend's equivalent of express.json() — it takes the raw JSON text your server sent back and converts it into a usable JavaScript object, so you can do things like result.message or result.data.

So the full round trip looks like:

Frontend → Server: JSON.stringify() (object → JSON text) + Content-Type label → sent
Server receives: express.json() middleware (JSON text → object) → now usable as req.body
Server → Frontend: res.json({...}) (Express automatically stringifies your object into JSON text + sets Content-Type on the response)
Frontend receives: res.json() (JSON text → object) → now usable as result.message, etc.

Both sides do both jobs — packaging outgoing data, and unpacking incoming data — just in opposite directions from each other. Does this clear up which side does what?